# Rust
#
# `**/target/`, not `/target/` (mika#2503). In a Cargo workspace every crate
# shares the root `target/`, so the anchored form was correct for the build —
# and it is the artefacts a PILOT leaves behind that it did not cover. PR #2502
# carried 28 files, 26 of them `.v1probe/target/**` (rlib, `incremental/`,
# fingerprints) from a scratch probe crate the pilot built at a non-root path;
# `/target/` never matched it, the mika#1282 rescue's `git add -A` obeyed
# .gitignore exactly and had been told nothing about that path, and QA blocked
# the result 7/7 for zero implementation. Same mechanism as #2486 the day before.
#
# MEASURED before changing it (2026-09-23): `git ls-files | grep -E '(^|/)target/'`
# returns ZERO paths across 3694 tracked files, so this rule cannot swallow
# anything that is tracked — it can only ever match an artefact.
#
# THE EFFECT SOUGHT IS ON THE PROBE, not just on the commit, and it is the same
# mechanism already written below for `/pr-body.md`: `git status --porcelain` —
# which is what `_rescue_dirty_worktree` interrogates to decide a worktree is
# dirty — DOES NOT REPORT IGNORED FILES. So a pilot that leaves only build
# artefacts behind no longer triggers a rescue at all, and `git add -A` is
# repaired without being touched, along with all five sites that consume
# RESCUE_EXCLUDE_PATHSPEC.
#
# DO NOT express this rule as a git pathspec instead — that is the form the next
# editor will reach for first, and it is silently destructive. Measured on this
# repo: `':(glob)!**/src/**'` leaves 0 of 3694 files (it excludes EVERYTHING,
# which would throw away the pilot's content), and `':!*src/*'` leaves 3203
# because `*` traverses slashes and so over-matches `docs/target/`, `mytarget/`,
# `retarget/`. Only .gitignore gives `**/` the "match in all directories"
# semantics the spec defines and this rule needs.
**/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local
.env.production

