# `.iterate/` — dispatch-lib rescue-path scratch (findings, escalate markers).
# Written by dev-groom/dev-pilot iteration loop as ephemeral session state;
# NEVER meant to persist. Committing these files causes rebase conflicts on
# every stale branch touched by dispatch-lib after a fresh iteration run
# (mika#1705 blocked 2× on this class, 41 commits behind).
.iterate/

# `pr-body.md` — the PR body a pilot writes at the worktree root before
# `gh pr create --body-file` (mika#2211). It is deleted right after, but the
# ignore is what makes the residue harmless: `git status --porcelain` — the
# dirty-worktree probe dispatch-lib runs — does not report ignored files, so a
# failed `gh pr create` that leaves the file behind can no longer trip the
# mika#1282 wip-rescue recovery it was written to avoid.
/pr-body.md

# `.tmp-main-check/` — a scratch worktree of `main` a pilot checks out inside
# its own worktree to compare its work against the base branch. Its `.git` is a
# FILE pointing at `.git/worktrees/<name>`, so while the worktree is registered
# the parent git ignores it wholesale and nothing here was needed. The failure
# is what happens AFTER it is pruned: `git worktree prune` — or the terminal
# reaper (mika#2420) and the target purge (mika#2497) — drops those metadata,
# and the directory degrades into an ordinary pile of files. The mika#1282
# wip-rescue's `git add -A` then swallows it whole, obeying .gitignore exactly
# and having been told nothing about this path. Measured on PR #2543 (mika#2506):
# 3733 files, ~972K insertions committed over a 10-file implementation, which is
# the third occurrence of this class after `.v1probe/target/**` (#2502) and #2486.
#
# NOT anchored (`.tmp-main-check/`, never `/.tmp-main-check/`), for the reason
# written on `**/target/` above: the anchored form is the one the next editor
# reaches for, and it is exactly what let `.v1probe/target/` through. A pilot
# that puts its scratch checkout at a non-root path is covered by this form and
# would not be by the other. Over-matching is not a risk — measured before
# writing this (2026-09-26): `git ls-files | grep tmp-main-check` returns
# nothing outside the artefact itself.
#
# AND THE EFFECT SOUGHT IS ON THE PROBE, not just on the commit — same mechanism
# as `/pr-body.md` right above: `git status --porcelain`, which is what
# `_rescue_dirty_worktree` interrogates to decide a worktree is dirty, DOES NOT
# REPORT IGNORED FILES. So a pilot that leaves this residue behind no longer
# triggers a rescue at all, and `git add -A` is repaired without being touched.
.tmp-main-check/

# CE review-run artifacts (ne jamais committer)
.ce-review-run/
